<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calc-papers.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calc-papers-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calc-papers.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="749" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim terms glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Property Insurance Claim Terms Glossary</h1><p class="lede">42 terms you’ll meet in Pennsylvania policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count>42 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Glossary letters"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="g-item" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the balance after repairs are done.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while your home is unlivable after a covered loss, often part of Coverage D.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss — the question an appraisal panel is normally limited to deciding.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by at least two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-B"><dt>Bad faith</dt><dd>Unreasonable conduct by an insurer toward its insured. Pennsylvania’s bad-faith statute lets a court award certain remedies; appraisal does not decide it.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Building code upgrade (ordinance or law)</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs needed to bring repaired work up to current building codes, often subject to its own limit.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-C"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage for the house itself and attached structures.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage for detached garages, fences, sheds and similar buildings.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property coverage for furniture, clothing, electronics and other belongings.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage D</dt><dd>Loss of use coverage, including additional living expense or fair rental value.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-D"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy showing the insured, property, coverages, limits, deductibles and policy period.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss you pay before the insurer pays, subtracted from the claim payment.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and condition, used to calculate actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial stake in the outcome beyond a reasonable fee, as most appraisal clauses require.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-E"><dt>Earth movement exclusion</dt><dd>Policy language that excludes damage from earthquakes, landslides, subsidence, sinkholes and similar ground movement, with limited exceptions.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-F"><dt>FAIR Plan</dt><dd>The Pennsylvania FAIR Plan, the state’s insurer of last resort for property owners who can’t get coverage in the regular market.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Flood exclusion</dt><dd>Standard homeowner policy language excluding damage from surface water and rising water; flood coverage comes from NFIP or private flood policies.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-H"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>The recoverable depreciation an insurer withholds until repairs are completed and documented.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-I"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps melting snow, which can back up under shingles and leak into the home.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as a quantity of drywall or roofing, with its unit price and total.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs reasonably match in color, texture or style.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Mine Subsidence Insurance (MSI)</dt><dd>A program run by the Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection that covers damage from the collapse of underground coal mines.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying out a wet basement.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-N"><dt>NFIP</dt><dd>The National Flood Insurance Program, the federal program that writes most flood insurance policies.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-O"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charges for overhead and profit, commonly added when a repair needs coordination of several trades.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-P"><dt>Party wall</dt><dd>A shared wall between two attached homes, common in rowhomes and twins, which can complicate repair scope and responsibility.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Pennsylvania Insurance Department</dt><dd>The state agency, led by the Insurance Commissioner, that regulates insurers, takes consumer complaints and licenses public adjusters.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which some policies require within a set time after the insurer asks.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A professional licensed by the Pennsylvania Insurance Department to prepare and negotiate a claim on the policyholder’s behalf for a fee.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation that can be paid back once repairs or replacement are completed under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without a deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which an insurer handles a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Roof square</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The full description of damaged areas and the repairs needed, separate from pricing.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Statement of loss</dt><dd>The insurer’s summary of the claim showing RCV, depreciation, deductible, prior payments and the net amount paid.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Suit-limitation clause</dt><dd>Policy language limiting the time to sue the insurer, commonly one year from the date of loss in Pennsylvania property policies.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an estimate after hidden damage is found or pricing changes.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Unfair claims practices</dt><dd>Claim-handling conduct the Unfair Insurance Practices Act and related regulations prohibit, such as failing to respond or investigate promptly.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-W"><dt>Wind-driven rain</dt><dd>Rain forced into a building by wind; coverage often depends on whether the wind first created an opening.</dd></div><div class="g-item" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers, adjusters and contractors to price property repairs.</dd></div></dl><p class="gloss-empty" hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article>
